WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:01.960
(host)
Maybe it's the unblinking eyes

00:00:02.043 --> 00:00:03.920
staring at you
emotionless day and night.

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.005
Or perhaps it's
their small size acting

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:07.882
as a cloak of innocence,

00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:09.634
or their human-like
appearance that makes them

00:00:09.718 --> 00:00:11.553
seem just slightly too real.

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:12.846
However you want to spin it,

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:14.848
there's something
creepy about dolls.

00:00:14.931 --> 00:00:17.392
Why do we take these
seemingly innocuous things

00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:18.685
and give them such life?

00:00:18.768 --> 00:00:20.228
And why do we continue
to imagine them

00:00:20.311 --> 00:00:22.063
as capable of evil and violence?

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:23.565
[upbeat music]

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:27.861
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:29.487
As imaginary others,

00:00:29.571 --> 00:00:31.865
it's easy to ascribe meaning
onto the artificial body.

00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:33.992
In addition to ritual
and religious use,

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:36.786
dolls might have been
the very first toys.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.496
Archeological evidence shows us

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.040
that the ancient Egyptians,
Chinese, Minoans,

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:44.878
and Etruscans all produce
dolls for spiritual purposes.

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:46.421
The oldest we have found so far

00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:48.715
dates to the third millennium.

00:00:48.798 --> 00:00:50.175
While early dolls
were sometimes used

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:52.469
for sinister purposes
much more frequently,

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.137
they were used for protection.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.264
In the third century BC
the use of dolls

00:00:56.347 --> 00:00:58.767
in spiritual practice seems
to take a negative turn,

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.143
at least in literature.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.811
In the second Idyll collection

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.898
of the Greek poet Theocritus,
part of the narrative details

00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:08.318
a witch using a wax doll
and a nefarious love spell.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.904
Roman poet Horace gives
a similar malevolent cast

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:14.157
in a 30 BCE text
in a story of necromancy

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.492
and erotic magic,

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:17.577
where two female witches
use a woolen doll

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.997
and a smaller wax doll
in a mock execution.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.749
Stereotypes and
demonizing rumors

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:25.668
about religious and spiritual
dolls like voodoo dolls,

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.837
kachinas, corn dollies,
and puppets

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:31.299
have undoubtedly contributed
to negative associations,

00:01:31.382 --> 00:01:33.218
particularly their
false connections

00:01:33.301 --> 00:01:36.095
to demonic witchcraft
and spirit manipulation.

00:01:36.179 --> 00:01:39.015
Views of spiritual practices
that use figurines or dolls

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:41.810
as conduits or ceremonial
figures negatively

00:01:41.893 --> 00:01:44.562
may have bled into cultural
attitudes towards dolls

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:47.106
designed for entertainment
and the consumer market.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.651
Human miniatures first
served ritual religious

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:51.361
and funerary purposes.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:53.071
But beginning in
medieval Europe,

00:01:53.154 --> 00:01:56.199
miniature fashion dolls were
collected by wealthy adults.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:59.285
Doll maker became a profession
by the early 15th century,

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:01.037
and doll houses
emerged in Europe

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.789
around the 16th century.

00:02:02.872 --> 00:02:05.124
When the fashions they wore
fell out of favor,

00:02:05.208 --> 00:02:08.169
the dolls might be passed on
to children as play things.

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:09.587
This history contributes

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:11.798
to an uncertainty and
unease about dolls.

00:02:11.881 --> 00:02:15.468
We aren't sure of their purpose,
at least not entirely.

00:02:15.552 --> 00:02:17.720
Dolls have always been
in-between objects

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:19.931
and that makes us uncomfortable.

00:02:20.014 --> 00:02:21.140
There's something about a doll

00:02:21.224 --> 00:02:22.642
that can give off a creepy vibe,

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:25.520
especially in recent times,
but why?

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:26.813
The first ventriloquist dummy

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:28.815
shows up around
the 18th century.

00:02:28.898 --> 00:02:30.066
I know technically
it's not a doll

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:32.527
but a puppet,
according to enthusiasts,

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:33.778
but they don't fall far

00:02:33.862 --> 00:02:36.281
from the uncanny valley
family doll tree.

00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:38.700
These dolls come alive
with movement and a voice.

00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:41.411
A ventriloquist's
performance is in part

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:44.747
dependent on the audience's
willingness to suspend disbelief

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:46.875
and see the doll as
a sentient person,

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:48.918
or at least detached
from the human operating.

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:52.422
It often made to look like
a child or child sized.

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:55.800
The ventriloquist dummy calls
to mind a youth possessed.

00:02:55.884 --> 00:02:57.969
Baby dolls and porcelain
dolls became popular

00:02:58.052 --> 00:02:59.554
in the 19th century,

00:02:59.637 --> 00:03:02.015
followed closely by socketed
joints, movable eyes,

00:03:02.098 --> 00:03:03.558
and talking dolls.

00:03:03.641 --> 00:03:05.310
What was also developing
around this time

00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:08.062
the modern concept of childhood.

00:03:08.146 --> 00:03:09.355
Before the
Enlightenment Movement

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:10.899
of the 17th and 18th century,

00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:12.609
many people just
didn't subscribe

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:14.694
to the cultural
concept of childhood.

00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:17.071
It was only when philosophers
presented the idea

00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:20.283
that human experience
defined knowledge and reason,

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:21.910
that people really
began to consider

00:03:21.993 --> 00:03:24.829
cultivating an ideal childhood
for personal development.

00:03:24.913 --> 00:03:26.956
Children became
seen as blank slates,

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.917
which meant that what
they played with mattered.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.293
This is when dolls become part

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.878
of children's
developmental play,

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:34.714
and it's when things really
take a creepier turn.

00:03:34.797 --> 00:03:36.507
So another reason
for our fear of dolls

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:37.884
is partially about the weariness

00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:40.261
of the child
and of childhood itself.

00:03:40.345 --> 00:03:42.972
Children, like dolls,
seem innocent and naive,

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:45.558
but we can't really know
what they're thinking.

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:49.354
Dolls when seen as tiny humans
emphasize this same point.

00:03:49.437 --> 00:03:50.688
This attitude becomes apparent

00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:52.273
as literature starts to emerge

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:54.859
blurring the boundary
between child and doll,

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:57.862
questioning what is
a living thing and what is not.

00:03:57.946 --> 00:03:59.656
Just look at "Pinocchio."

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:02.992
First published as a magazine
serial in 1881 and 1882

00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:05.870
and collected into
a single novel in 1883,

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:07.956
"The Adventures of
Pinocchio" follows the story

00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:11.542
of a marionette doll crafted
from a talking piece of wood

00:04:11.626 --> 00:04:13.795
that wept and
laughed like a child,

00:04:13.878 --> 00:04:15.880
a terrifying little
tidbit excluded

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:17.674
from the Disney
version of the story.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:19.342
Oh, and Jiminy Cricket.

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:20.885
Yeah, in the original story

00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:23.471
Pinocchio kills the talking
cricket with a hammer,

00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:25.682
and that's only the tip
of the creepy iceberg

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:27.517
and the unintentional
horror story.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.102
Before becoming a real boy,

00:04:29.185 --> 00:04:31.938
the doll experiences some
pretty traumatic stuff,

00:04:32.021 --> 00:04:33.648
contributing
to the story's questioning

00:04:33.731 --> 00:04:35.650
of both morality and humanity.

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:37.944
While Pinocchio
the talking sentient marionette

00:04:38.027 --> 00:04:40.279
was firmly grounded
in the pages of fiction,

00:04:40.363 --> 00:04:42.532
real-world dolls were
becoming more human-like

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:44.826
by the addition of voices.

00:04:44.909 --> 00:04:47.453
The end of the 19th century
gave us the first talking dolls

00:04:47.537 --> 00:04:49.372
from an unlikely source.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.916
After inventing the
phonograph in 1877,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.252
Thomas Edison invented
a talking doll

00:04:54.335 --> 00:04:56.129
who got her voice
from a tiny version

00:04:56.212 --> 00:04:57.839
of his earlier invention.

00:04:57.922 --> 00:04:59.507
While Edison thought
the doll would be a hit

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.927
in the late 1880s,
he was grossly mistaken.

00:05:03.011 --> 00:05:05.805
Dolls bridge the boundary
between child and adult.

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:07.473
Sometimes they have
adult-like features

00:05:07.557 --> 00:05:09.183
on a child-sized body.

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:11.060
There's also the way
kids play with them,

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.979
caring for them like
children of their own

00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:15.898
or crafting fictional
adult lives to act out.

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.567
It blurs the child

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.318
and the adult too much
for us to be comfortable with,

00:05:19.402 --> 00:05:21.779
and we're right back
to that uncanny valley.

00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:23.990
Take the infamous
Robert the Doll.

00:05:24.073 --> 00:05:26.159
Gifted to Robert Eugene Otto
by his grandfather

00:05:26.242 --> 00:05:30.288
around 1904, the doll became
the boy's constant companion.

00:05:30.371 --> 00:05:33.249
He talked to the doll and
treated it like a real person.

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:35.376
He even gave the doll
his name, Robert,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.129
and began calling himself Gene.

00:05:38.212 --> 00:05:42.383
Human Gene blamed mischief and
accidents on Robert, the doll.

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:44.802
Part of a wealthy and eccentric
family in Key West,

00:05:44.886 --> 00:05:47.847
rumors about Gene
and the doll flourished.

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:50.850
Robert stayed a part of Gene's
life into his adulthood.

00:05:50.933 --> 00:05:53.144
Children would report seeing
the doll standing by himself

00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:56.564
in the window of Gene's house,
seeming to move on its own.

00:05:56.647 --> 00:05:58.775
After Gene's death,
the new owner of the house

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:02.487
and the doll reported hearing
footsteps and giggling.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.447
She also saw the facial
expression change

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.824
if anyone spoke poorly of Gene.

00:06:06.908 --> 00:06:08.409
Many creators have
used the story

00:06:08.493 --> 00:06:11.579
as inspiration for all
kinds of terrifying stories,

00:06:11.662 --> 00:06:13.623
but none as famous as Chucky--

00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:15.541
the killer doll that
launched a franchise

00:06:15.625 --> 00:06:17.960
and gave pretty much
everyone nightmares.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.880
Hi, I'm Chucky. Wanna play?

00:06:20.963 --> 00:06:22.590
[screams]

00:06:22.673 --> 00:06:23.925
So living dolls are getting

00:06:24.008 --> 00:06:25.468
the public's interest
in literature

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:28.638
and eccentric real world tales
in the early 20th century,

00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:31.015
and film
wouldn't be far behind.

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:33.726
Dolls firmly entered
the horror genre in 1945,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.395
the important British
horror anthology

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:38.856
"Dead of Night" ends
with the creepy tale

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.651
of the relationship between
a ventriloquist and his dummy--

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:44.278
a relationship that
questioned who was the master

00:06:44.362 --> 00:06:47.448
and who was the puppet,
and who was the murderer.

00:06:47.532 --> 00:06:49.408
A few decades later,
the "Twilight Zone"

00:06:49.492 --> 00:06:50.868
a series that does
excellent work

00:06:50.952 --> 00:06:52.078
turning the mundane terrifying,

00:06:52.161 --> 00:06:54.705
aired a standout episode
that took a doll

00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:56.999
from merely creepy to evil.

00:06:57.083 --> 00:06:59.585
In "Living Doll," Annabelle
buys her daughter Christie

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:02.338
a Talky Tina in an attempt
to distract Christie

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:05.258
from the harsh parenting
of her new stepfather.

00:07:05.341 --> 00:07:07.552
The thing is,
Talky Tina isn't happy

00:07:07.635 --> 00:07:09.929
about Christie's mistreatment,
and the doll begins

00:07:10.012 --> 00:07:13.307
to move on her own tormenting
and threatening the stepfather

00:07:13.391 --> 00:07:15.434
before eventually killing him.

00:07:15.518 --> 00:07:18.688
The episode's final words
come from the doll herself.

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:21.023
In 1969, "Playboy"
got into the doll game,

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:23.359
but not probably
in the way you're thinking.

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:25.027
Famous science fiction
and horror writer

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:27.822
Richard Matheson published
a short story "Prey"

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:29.448
in the April edition.

00:07:29.532 --> 00:07:31.576
In the story,
a woman named Amelia

00:07:31.659 --> 00:07:33.953
buys a Zuni fetish doll
named He Who Kills

00:07:34.036 --> 00:07:36.247
as a birthday gift
for her boyfriend.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.292
Described as "seven inches
long and carved from wood

00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:42.378
"with a skeletal body,
oversized head, pointed teeth,

00:07:42.461 --> 00:07:46.174
"bulging eyes, and a tiny gold
chain wrapped around its body,

00:07:46.257 --> 00:07:48.551
keeping the spirit
inside from escaping."

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:50.553
When the doll falls
from the edge of a table,

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:52.096
the chain slips off.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.056
The doll stalks
and hunts Amelia

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.309
with supernatural speed
and ferocity,

00:07:56.392 --> 00:07:58.728
injuring and eventually
possessing her.

00:07:58.811 --> 00:08:00.521
And this horrific variation,

00:08:00.605 --> 00:08:03.232
the doll's malevolence is
formed from a misunderstanding

00:08:03.316 --> 00:08:04.734
of Zuni practices.

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:06.736
The story took on new
life when it was adapted

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:10.865
for television as "Amelia" in
the 1975 "Trilogy of Terror."

00:08:10.948 --> 00:08:14.202
The doll's physical appearance
is significant in this time.

00:08:14.285 --> 00:08:16.287
It is muscular,
shirtless, and black,

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.663
a racist take on the tale

00:08:17.747 --> 00:08:20.208
as the doll stalks
the white Amelia.

00:08:20.291 --> 00:08:23.127
In the sequel, "He Who Kills"
from the "Trilogy of Terror II,"

00:08:23.211 --> 00:08:25.671
the same doll hunts
a white Dr. Simpson

00:08:25.755 --> 00:08:28.257
and now has a cannibalistic
need for flesh--

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:30.801
another damaging
and false stereotype.

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:34.555
The 1970s marked a return to
the creepy ventriloquist dummy.

00:08:34.639 --> 00:08:36.432
In William Goldman's
novel "Magic,"

00:08:36.515 --> 00:08:38.684
a struggling magician
buys a foulmouthed dummy

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:40.603
that may or may not be alive.

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:43.272
A story of suspense,
the reader is left wondering

00:08:43.356 --> 00:08:47.151
if the magician Corky is really
a violent mentally ill man

00:08:47.235 --> 00:08:50.488
or if the dummy fats is
behind the murders he commits.

00:08:50.571 --> 00:08:51.781
Anthony Hopkins would star

00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:54.575
in the 1978 film
adaptation that gives us

00:08:54.659 --> 00:08:58.120
yet another truly creepy doll
for our nightmares.

00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.665
It also marks a change from
the demonic possession trope

00:09:00.748 --> 00:09:03.960
and into conversations
about psychopathy and trauma--

00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:06.504
topics under scrutiny as
a handful of serial killers

00:09:06.587 --> 00:09:08.422
are discovered across the globe,

00:09:08.506 --> 00:09:10.841
including John Wayne Gacy
and Ted Bundy.

00:09:10.925 --> 00:09:13.761
And in 1982, "Poltergeist"
just really puts the cherry

00:09:13.844 --> 00:09:16.222
on top of
the child monster doll deal.

00:09:16.305 --> 00:09:18.391
Robbie's murderous
clown doll manages

00:09:18.474 --> 00:09:21.185
to combine two terrifying
monsters-- the creepy doll

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:24.105
and the killer clown--
into one epic jump scare.

00:09:24.188 --> 00:09:25.982
It gets me
every time I watch it--

00:09:26.065 --> 00:09:27.775
literally every time.

00:09:27.858 --> 00:09:30.278
[evil doll cackling]

00:09:30.361 --> 00:09:31.737
And I think one of the reasons

00:09:31.821 --> 00:09:33.781
it's so incredibly
effective as a monster

00:09:33.864 --> 00:09:36.284
is because it strikes when
Robbie should be sleeping--

00:09:36.367 --> 00:09:38.035
snuggling safe in his room,

00:09:38.119 --> 00:09:41.038
his mother just down the hall,
relaxing in the bath.

00:09:41.122 --> 00:09:43.541
Dolls often reside in
playrooms and bedrooms.

00:09:43.624 --> 00:09:46.919
Spaces in the home meant to
be safe havens for children.

00:09:47.003 --> 00:09:49.880
But sometimes real human
monsters violate that safety

00:09:49.964 --> 00:09:52.466
and the evil doll can be
a metaphor for that offense.

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:54.010
The 1980s also brought

00:09:54.093 --> 00:09:57.346
one of the most iconic killer
dolls in history-- Chucky.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:58.973
"Child's Play" takes
a serial killer

00:09:59.056 --> 00:10:01.600
and places his ghost in
the body of a talking doll,

00:10:01.684 --> 00:10:02.893
literally bringing
the killer into the home,

00:10:02.977 --> 00:10:05.604
disguised as
an object of comfort.

00:10:05.688 --> 00:10:07.648
In an additional
element of horror,

00:10:07.732 --> 00:10:10.234
while the audience knows that
the doll is the real killer

00:10:10.318 --> 00:10:12.987
within the world of the movie,
the police are convinced

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:16.032
that Chucky's owner,
a child, is the murderer,

00:10:16.115 --> 00:10:17.867
presumably driven
to commit violence

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.411
because of the trauma
of losing his father.

00:10:20.494 --> 00:10:21.954
If you're a parent,
it can often seem

00:10:22.038 --> 00:10:23.581
like children have a death wish,

00:10:23.664 --> 00:10:26.250
and it's not that far a mental
leap to imagine the inverse

00:10:26.334 --> 00:10:28.169
of that murderous intention.

00:10:28.252 --> 00:10:30.421
I'm looking at you,
sleep deprivation.

00:10:30.504 --> 00:10:32.173
Chucky launched
a successful franchise

00:10:32.256 --> 00:10:35.134
that served up scares
alongside social commentary.

00:10:35.217 --> 00:10:36.802
Chucky's sequels are a satire

00:10:36.886 --> 00:10:39.513
on heteronormativity that
challenge the very concept

00:10:39.597 --> 00:10:43.309
of life by disrupting and
questioning conception itself.

00:10:43.392 --> 00:10:45.978
In "Bride of Chucky,"
female doll Tiffany gives birth

00:10:46.062 --> 00:10:47.605
to a doll child.

00:10:47.688 --> 00:10:49.982
"Seed of Chucky" continues
the story of Tiffany's child,

00:10:50.066 --> 00:10:52.193
who has ambiguously
gendered and sexed,

00:10:52.276 --> 00:10:54.528
and on a journey to
find their doll parents.

00:10:54.612 --> 00:10:55.863
When this goal is achieved,

00:10:55.946 --> 00:10:58.866
the child, alternatively
called Glen and Glenda,

00:10:58.949 --> 00:11:00.910
brings their parents back
to life with a ritual,

00:11:00.993 --> 00:11:03.537
essentially rebirthing
their own parents.

00:11:03.621 --> 00:11:04.830
Then the dolls capture
actress Jennifer Tilly,

00:11:04.914 --> 00:11:08.125
playing herself, who
is forcibly impregnated

00:11:08.209 --> 00:11:09.668
with Chucky Seed and gives birth

00:11:09.752 --> 00:11:12.213
to human twins who
are then possessed

00:11:12.296 --> 00:11:13.964
by the spirit of the doll child,

00:11:14.048 --> 00:11:15.883
who I guess was
born with two souls--

00:11:15.966 --> 00:11:17.593
one male and one female.

00:11:17.676 --> 00:11:19.345
But yeah, the movie was marketed

00:11:19.428 --> 00:11:22.932
at gay pride parades alongside
special Chucky condoms.

00:11:23.015 --> 00:11:25.142
The "Child's Play" remake
brought attention back

00:11:25.226 --> 00:11:27.561
to the franchise in 2019,

00:11:27.645 --> 00:11:29.480
but it was really the
"Chucky" television series

00:11:29.563 --> 00:11:32.316
that continued the
franchise's queer history.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.360
So this doll is made into
to a monstrous other

00:11:34.443 --> 00:11:37.488
that challenges human
relationships and norms.

00:11:37.571 --> 00:11:39.365
Another part of the fear
of dolls, I believe,

00:11:39.448 --> 00:11:42.368
comes from conceptualizing
them as empty human vessels

00:11:42.451 --> 00:11:44.203
we can ascribe meaning to.

00:11:44.286 --> 00:11:45.788
A child plays with the doll

00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:47.706
and makes up a whole history
for their play thing.

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:49.917
But there's a fear that
the doll will come alive

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.129
with a vengeance, unhappy
with the life it's been given.

00:11:53.212 --> 00:11:54.755
The interesting
array of creepy dolls

00:11:54.839 --> 00:11:57.716
from the 1990s
highlights this anxiety.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.261
A killer ventriloquist dummy
in "Tales from The Crypt"

00:12:00.344 --> 00:12:01.887
is revealed to be
the ventriloquist's

00:12:01.971 --> 00:12:04.056
murderous Siamese twin.

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.934
Slappy from 1993's "Goosebumps:
Night of the Living Dummy,"

00:12:07.017 --> 00:12:08.894
inspired in part
by R. L. Stine's fear

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:10.771
of the original Pinocchio story,

00:12:10.855 --> 00:12:12.481
is a sadistic dummy who fights

00:12:12.565 --> 00:12:14.650
another animated dummy,
Mr. Wood.

00:12:14.733 --> 00:12:16.318
The two girls who own the puppet

00:12:16.402 --> 00:12:18.362
spend much of the book
trying to convince the adults

00:12:18.446 --> 00:12:20.281
around them that
the dolls are alive

00:12:20.364 --> 00:12:23.701
and that Mr. Wood is trying
to make them his slaves.

00:12:23.784 --> 00:12:25.369
The idea that dolls
could be alive

00:12:25.453 --> 00:12:28.497
took a much more PG turn
in 1995 with "Toy Story."

00:12:28.581 --> 00:12:29.915
Well, kind of.

00:12:29.999 --> 00:12:32.460
The vengeful, mutilated,
and tortured dolls of Sid

00:12:32.543 --> 00:12:34.420
swarm the boy
in a scene that seems

00:12:34.503 --> 00:12:36.046
straight out of a horror movie,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.257
complete with exorcist
spinning head reference

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:39.925
and zombie-like toy soldiers.

00:12:40.009 --> 00:12:41.552
(Sid)
Ahh!

00:12:41.635 --> 00:12:43.804
I know this movie is
why I became hyper aware

00:12:43.888 --> 00:12:45.806
of how I was
treating all my toys

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.517
for fear that they would come
alive at night and attack me.

00:12:48.601 --> 00:12:50.019
The 21st century is certainly

00:12:50.102 --> 00:12:52.271
not lacking in
scary doll stories.

00:12:52.354 --> 00:12:53.856
The uncanny faces
of dolls appear

00:12:53.939 --> 00:12:56.317
in massive horror
franchises from "Saw"

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:59.195
to "The Conjuring Universe,"
and most recently, "M3gan."

00:12:59.278 --> 00:13:02.072
All of these stories
explore family, faith,

00:13:02.156 --> 00:13:04.742
and the lingering effects
of acts deemed sinful

00:13:04.825 --> 00:13:07.786
either spiritually,
socially, or intellectually.

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:09.413
The dolls and all of them act

00:13:09.497 --> 00:13:12.583
as surprising containers for
violent and deviant behavior.

00:13:12.666 --> 00:13:14.168
The doll becomes a monster

00:13:14.251 --> 00:13:16.795
only because of a human's
fear, the terror of childhood,

00:13:16.879 --> 00:13:19.840
the possibility of malevolent
intent in a small body,

00:13:19.924 --> 00:13:22.551
the misguided intentions
of burgeoning identity.

00:13:22.635 --> 00:13:24.178
It is our discomfort

00:13:24.261 --> 00:13:26.805
with the uncannyness of their
humanness that unnerves us,

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:28.891
especially when there are
artificial motivations

00:13:28.974 --> 00:13:31.644
to closely resemble
our own dark urges.

00:13:34.688 --> 00:13:36.065
Hey, friend.

00:13:37.358 --> 00:13:40.069
Before you go, we want
to hear your feedback.

00:13:40.152 --> 00:13:43.113
Every year PBS Digital
Studios surveys our audience

00:13:43.197 --> 00:13:45.658
helping us understand
what you enjoy on YouTube

00:13:45.741 --> 00:13:46.992
and what you want us
to make more of.

00:13:47.076 --> 00:13:49.620
You also get to vote
on new show ideas,

00:13:49.703 --> 00:13:51.372
so it'd be great
if "Monstrum" fans

00:13:51.455 --> 00:13:52.873
were represented in the polls.

00:13:52.957 --> 00:13:54.250
There's a link in
the description.

00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:55.960
Thanks in advance.

00:13:56.043 --> 00:13:57.586
The doll becomes an-- oh.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:13:59.088
[crew speaking]

00:13:59.171 --> 00:14:01.882
Ah! Aah!

00:14:01.966 --> 00:14:02.758
Hey.

00:14:02.841 --> 00:14:05.177
Ah! [laughs]

